1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,519
LiveReload flickers on the museum ticket
purchase page for the third time in a
minute. A designer changed CSS. DevTools
restarted the context. The WebSocket
reload hits before Thymeleaf finishes
compiling. Another save.

2
00:00:15,639 --> 00:00:23,479
Another restart. The developer disables
LiveReload in frustration, then wonders
why classpath changes no longer restart
anything.

3
00:00:23,599 --> 00:00:31,839
DevTools is a power tool for local
feedback — not a production dependency,
and not magic that understands design-tool
save storms on a ticket UI.

4
00:00:31,959 --> 00:00:43,599
Spring Boot DevTools watches the classpath
for changes, triggers a fast application
restart using two classloaders (base jars
stay loaded; project classes reload), and
can push browser LiveReload events.

5
00:00:43,719 --> 00:00:56,320
It disables some caching for templates in
development. Exclude the dependency from
production builds — optional Maven
dependency and runtimeOnly patterns exist
so the fat jar you ship does not carry the
restart agent.

6
00:00:56,439 --> 00:01:09,319
Look at this example. <dependency> Look at
this example. # application-local.yml Walk
the local config. thymeleaf.cache: false
ensures template edits are visible without
relying only on restart.

7
00:01:09,439 --> 00:01:16,439
livereload.enabled: true opens the
LiveReload server that browsers (with an
extension or injected script) listen to.

8
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:29,400
restart.additional-exclude:
static/,public/ is the museum fix: CSS and
static asset saves should *not* trigger a
full application restart — only a browser
refresh if LiveReload remains on.

9
00:01:29,519 --> 00:01:42,560
Java and template class changes still
restart the context.
<optional>true</optional> helps keep
DevTools from being pulled transitively
into downstream modules. Runtime while
building the museum UI.

10
00:01:42,679 --> 00:01:57,879
Save a Java controller → DevTools detects
classpath change → restart classloader
discards project classes and rebuilds the
ApplicationContext faster than a cold JVM
start, but still re-runs bean creation,
@PostConstruct,

11
00:01:57,999 --> 00:01:59,479
and connection setup.

12
00:01:59,599 --> 00:02:07,199
Save a static CSS file under an excluded
path → no restart; LiveReload can refresh
the browser alone.

13
00:02:07,319 --> 00:02:15,439
Leave static files inside the restart
trigger set and every CSS save pays a full
context reboot — the flicker loop that
burned the designer.

14
00:02:15,559 --> 00:02:23,559
Remote DevTools exists for updating a
remote app; treat it as a security surface
requiring secrets, not a default for
shared environments.

15
00:02:23,679 --> 00:02:36,599
Failure mode symptoms: context restart
thrash — logs show repeated "Restarting
application" on every autosave from an IDE
or design tool writing static files;
browser flashes white;

16
00:02:36,719 --> 00:02:44,199
in-progress form state on the ticket page
resets; local H2 database re-creates empty
because the embedded bean was rebuilt.

17
00:02:44,319 --> 00:02:54,999
Another failure: DevTools accidentally on
the production classpath — unexpected
restart behavior or LiveReload port open;
always verify the production image's
dependency tree.

18
00:02:55,119 --> 00:03:06,599
Disabling LiveReload in the browser
extension while leaving restart triggers
wide still restarts on CSS if excludes are
wrong — people blame LiveReload for
restart cost when the restart watcher is
the real bill.

19
00:03:06,719 --> 00:03:20,999
Trade-offs. DevTools shortens the edit-
refresh loop for server-side UI work and
small API tweaks; it is not a substitute
for JRebel/HotSwap when you need true
method replacement without resetting
singletons.

20
00:03:21,119 --> 00:03:31,239
Stateful beans, in-memory databases, and
warm caches reset on every restart —
externalize local Postgres if you need
durable local data across restarts.

21
00:03:31,359 --> 00:03:44,839
Turn DevTools off when diagnosing timing
bugs that only appear under steady state.
Classloader split, briefly: the "base"
loader holds dependency jars that rarely
change; the "restart" loader holds your
target/classes.

22
00:03:44,959 --> 00:03:56,719
On restart, only the restart loader is
discarded and rebuilt, which is why
DevTools feels faster than killing the JVM
— Hibernate jars do not reload. Your
@Service singletons do.

23
00:03:56,839 --> 00:04:04,959
Museum ticket sessions stored only in an
in-memory bean vanish; designer CSS
excludes avoid paying that tax for static
edits.

24
00:04:05,079 --> 00:04:12,479
Knowing which loader owns a class explains
why a dependency bump still needs a full
stop/start while a controller tweak does
not.

25
00:04:12,599 --> 00:04:14,919
Misconception unique to DevTools:

26
00:04:15,039 --> 00:04:27,480
"DevTools restart is the same as hot-swap
/ HotSwap agent / JRebel class
redefinition." It is a restart of the
application context with a clever
classloader split — faster than killing
the JVM,

27
00:04:27,599 --> 00:04:30,759
slower and broader than swapping one
method body.

28
00:04:30,879 --> 00:04:39,239
Stateful beans reset. In-memory H2 re-
creates unless you externalize it. The
ticket UI iteration speed is acceptable
again.

29
00:04:39,359 --> 00:04:50,119
At 02:00 the ferry-booking on-call channel
pastes four log lines from four pods with
no shared request id — DevTools will not
help. Logging strategy and correlation
will.
